 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "eq:3.1.14",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "a-better-approach",
   "prompt": "<p>Show that the matter wave $\\kPsi(\\kx,\\kt)=\\exp\\left[i(\\kp\\kx-\\kE\\kt)/\\hbar\\right]$ satisfies the free-particle equation $i\\hbar\\,\\partial\\kPsi/\\partial\\kt = -(\\hbar^2/2\\km)\\,\\partial^2\\kPsi/\\partial\\kx^2$ when $\\kE = \\kp^2/2\\km$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Work out each side separately and write both as a number times $\\kPsi$.",
    "solution": "<p>Differentiating with respect to $\\kt$ brings down $-i\\kE/\\hbar$, so $i\\hbar\\,\\partial\\kPsi/\\partial\\kt = i\\hbar(-i\\kE/\\hbar)\\kPsi = \\kE\\kPsi$. Each derivative with respect to $\\kx$ brings down $i\\kp/\\hbar$, so $\\partial^2\\kPsi/\\partial\\kx^2 = (i\\kp/\\hbar)^2\\kPsi = -(\\kp^2/\\hbar^2)\\kPsi$, and the right side is $-(\\hbar^2/2\\km)(-\\kp^2/\\hbar^2)\\kPsi = (\\kp^2/2\\km)\\kPsi$. The two sides are $\\kE\\kPsi$ and $(\\kp^2/2\\km)\\kPsi$, which are equal because $\\kE = \\kp^2/2\\km$ for a free particle.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "eq:3.1.18",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "a-better-approach",
   "prompt": "<p>A particle is in a state of energy $\\kE = 1.0$ eV, so its wavefunction is $\\kPsi(\\krvec,\\kt)=\\kpsi(\\krvec)e^{-i\\kE\\kt/\\hbar}$. At what frequency does the phase factor $e^{-i\\kE\\kt/\\hbar}$ return to its starting value? ($1\\ \\text{eV} = 1.602\\times10^{-19}$ J, $h = 6.626\\times10^{-34}$ J s.)</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 242000000000000.0,
    "unit": "Hz",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "The phase $\\kE\\kt/\\hbar$ must grow by $2\\pi$.",
    "solution": "<p>The factor repeats when $\\kE\\kt/\\hbar$ grows by $2\\pi$, that is after a time $2\\pi\\hbar/\\kE = h/\\kE$. Its frequency is $\\knu = \\kE/h = (1.602\\times10^{-19}\\ \\text{J})/(6.626\\times10^{-34}\\ \\text{J s}) = 2.42\\times10^{14}$ Hz, the Planck relation $\\kE = h\\knu$ again.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
